## 1. Selected dimensions

Assumptions: uniform gross rectangular section, centered axial tension, and no reduction for holes or connections.

Required gross area:

\[
A_{\rm req}=\frac{P}{\sigma_{\rm allow}}
=\frac{6.00\times10^3\ \mathrm{N}}{100\ \mathrm{N/mm^2}}
=60.0\ \mathrm{mm^2}
\]

Required thickness:

\[
t_{\rm req}=\frac{A_{\rm req}}{b}
=\frac{60.0}{20.0}
=3.00\ \mathrm{mm}
\]

Thus, the smallest available passing thickness is:

\[
\boxed{t=3.00\ \mathrm{mm}}
\]

Selected strip dimensions:

\[
\boxed{500\ \mathrm{mm}\times20.0\ \mathrm{mm}\times3.00\ \mathrm{mm}}
\]

## 2. Strength check

For the selected \(3.00\ \mathrm{mm}\) strip:

\[
A=bt=(20.0)(3.00)=60.0\ \mathrm{mm^2}
\]

\[
\sigma=\frac{P}{A}
=\frac{6.00\times10^3}{60.0}
=100\ \mathrm{MPa}
\]

Because equality with the allowable stress is permitted:

\[
\boxed{100\ \mathrm{MPa}\leq100\ \mathrm{MPa}\quad\text{(passes)}}
\]

For the next thinner available strip, \(t=2.00\ \mathrm{mm}\):

\[
A=(20.0)(2.00)=40.0\ \mathrm{mm^2}
\]

\[
\sigma=\frac{6.00\times10^3}{40.0}
=150\ \mathrm{MPa}
\]

\[
\boxed{150\ \mathrm{MPa}>100\ \mathrm{MPa}\quad\text{(fails)}}
\]

## 3. Volume and mass

Selected-strip volume:

\[
V=Lbt=(500)(20.0)(3.00)
=30{,}000\ \mathrm{mm^3}
\]

Since \(1\ \mathrm{m^3}=10^9\ \mathrm{mm^3}\):

\[
V=\frac{30{,}000}{10^9}
=3.00\times10^{-5}\ \mathrm{m^3}
\]

Mass:

\[
m=\rho V
=(7850)(3.00\times10^{-5})
=0.2355\ \mathrm{kg}
\]

\[
\boxed{V=3.00\times10^{-5}\ \mathrm{m^3}=30.0\ \mathrm{cm^3}}
\]

\[
\boxed{m=0.2355\ \mathrm{kg}\approx0.236\ \mathrm{kg}\;(235.5\ \mathrm{g})}
\]

## Final conclusion

Select the \(\boxed{500\ \mathrm{mm}\times20.0\ \mathrm{mm}\times3.00\ \mathrm{mm}}\) steel strip. Its axial stress is exactly \(\boxed{100\ \mathrm{MPa}}\), so it passes; the next thinner \(2.00\ \mathrm{mm}\) strip has \(\boxed{150\ \mathrm{MPa}}\) and fails. The selected strip has volume \(\boxed{3.00\times10^{-5}\ \mathrm{m^3}}\) and mass \(\boxed{0.2355\ \mathrm{kg}}\).
